<h1>Multiple Media & Memories System Documentation</h1>

<div class="doc-meta">
  <strong>System:</strong> Family Sanctuary Platform<br>
  <strong>Module:</strong> Owner Memories Vault & Media Management<br>
  <strong>Topics:</strong> Array Storage in Database, Resilient Promise.race Processing, API Response, UI Gallery, Details Page & Video Autoplay<br>
  <strong>Date Generated:</strong> September 20, 2026<br>
  <strong>Status:</strong> Implemented, Verified, Pushed to <code>main</code> and <code>kayes</code>
</div>

<h2>1. Executive Summary</h2>
<p>
  This document explains the architecture and implementation of the <strong>Multiple Media Memories System</strong>. 
  When the Sanctuary Owner adds memories to the family archive, they can attach multiple photos and videos simultaneously. 
  The system guarantees:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Array Storage in Database:</strong> Stored cleanly as a JSON array in PostgreSQL (<code>mediaUrls: string[]</code>).</li>
  <li><strong>Resilient Processing (Promise.race & try/catch):</strong> If any single media item fails or times out, the other files are safely preserved and saved.</li>
  <li><strong>API Array Return:</strong> All endpoints return <code>mediaUrls: string[]</code> so the frontend receives every attached file.</li>
  <li><strong>Dedicated Details Page (<code>/owner-dashboard/memories/[id]</code>):</strong> Displays the complete media gallery, metadata, and enables <strong>automatic video playback</strong>.</li>
  <li><strong>Interactive Lightbox:</strong> Supports photo and video viewing with a thumbnail switcher and autoplaying video player.</li>
</ul>

<h2>2. How Multiple Media Files Are Stored & Returned as an Array</h2>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">Database Storage Strategy</div>
  <p>
    PostgreSQL's <code>media_url</code> text column stores a serialized JSON array representing all uploaded photos and videos:
    <code>'["url1", "url2", "url3"]'</code>.
    The <code>photo_count</code> integer column is automatically synchronized with the length of the array.
  </p>
</div>

<h3>Backend Implementation (<code>family.service.ts</code>)</h3>
<p>In <code>src/family/family.service.ts</code>, we implemented two core helper methods:</p>

<pre><code>// 1. Parse stored string into array
private parseMediaUrls(mediaUrl: string | null): string[] {
  if (!mediaUrl) return [];
  try {
    const trimmed = mediaUrl.trim();
    if (trimmed.startsWith('[') && trimmed.endsWith(']')) {
      const parsed = JSON.parse(trimmed);
      if (Array.isArray(parsed)) {
        return parsed.filter((item) => typeof item === 'string' && item.length > 0);
      }
    }
    return [trimmed];
  } catch {
    return [mediaUrl];
  }
}

// 2. Resilient processing using Promise.race and try/catch
private async processMediaResiliently(mediaUrls?: string[], singleMediaUrl?: string): Promise&lt;string[]&gt; {
  const rawList: string[] = [];

  if (Array.isArray(mediaUrls) && mediaUrls.length > 0) {
    rawList.push(...mediaUrls.filter((u) => typeof u === 'string' && u.trim().length > 0));
  } else if (singleMediaUrl && singleMediaUrl.trim().length > 0) {
    rawList.push(...this.parseMediaUrls(singleMediaUrl));
  }

  if (rawList.length === 0) return [];

  // Each item is processed inside try/catch and raced against a timeout
  const results = await Promise.allSettled(
    rawList.map(async (itemUrl) => {
      try {
        return await Promise.race([
          Promise.resolve(itemUrl.trim()),
          new Promise&lt;string&gt;((_, reject) =>
            setTimeout(() => reject(new Error('Media validation timeout')), 4000),
          ),
        ]);
      } catch (err) {
        console.warn(`[Promise.race] Media item failed or timed out:`, err);
        return null; // Return null so other items still succeed!
      }
    }),
  );

  return results
    .filter((r): r is PromiseFulfilledResult&lt;string | null&gt; => r.status === 'fulfilled' && !!r.value)
    .map((r) => r.value as string);
}</code></pre>

<h3>API Response Guarantee</h3>
<p>
  When <code>getMemories()</code>, <code>getMemoryById(id)</code>, <code>createMemory(...)</code>, or <code>updateMemory(...)</code> execute, they return:
</p>
<pre><code>{
  id: "304a7b7c-...",
  title: "Eid al-Fitr Family Celebration 2026",
  description: "...",
  sharedBy: "Sanctuary Owner",
  photoCount: 3,
  mediaUrls: [
    "data:image/jpeg;base64,...",
    "https://example.com/video.mp4",
    "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-..."
  ],
  mediaUrl: "data:image/jpeg;base64,..." // Primary cover image for backward compatibility
}</code></pre>

<h2>3. Frontend Multiple Media Upload & Resilient File Reading</h2>

<p>In <code>src/app/owner-dashboard/memories/create/page.tsx</code>, files are read asynchronously with <code>Promise.race</code>:</p>

<pre><code>const readFileWithPromiseRace = (file: File): Promise&lt;string&gt; => {
  return Promise.race([
    new Promise&lt;string&gt;((resolve, reject) => {
      const reader = new FileReader();
      reader.onload = () => {
        if (typeof reader.result === "string") resolve(reader.result);
        else reject(new Error("File result is not a string"));
      };
      reader.onerror = () => reject(reader.error || new Error("Failed to read file"));
      reader.readAsDataURL(file);
    }),
    new Promise&lt;string&gt;((_, reject) =>
      setTimeout(() => reject(new Error(`Timeout reading file: ${file.name}`)), 5000)
    ),
  ]);
};</code></pre>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">Why this guarantees no files are lost:</div>
  <p>
    When selecting 5 files, if file #2 is corrupt or times out:
    the <code>try/catch</code> catches the error for file #2, logs a warning, and continues reading files #1, #3, #4, and #5. All valid files are added to <code>mediaUrls</code> and saved to the database.
  </p>
</div>

<h2>4. Dedicated Memory Details Page & Video Autoplay</h2>

<h3>Details Page Location</h3>
<p><code>src/app/owner-dashboard/memories/[id]/page.tsx</code></p>

<h3>Video Autoplay Implementation</h3>
<p>
  Browsers have strict autoplay policies: a video will only autoplay if it is <strong>muted</strong> and includes the <strong>playsInline</strong> attribute.
  Both the Details Page and the Lightbox Modal implement this standard:
</p>

<pre><code>&lt;video
  src={activeMediaUrl}
  controls
  autoPlay
  muted
  loop
  playsInline
  className="w-full h-full object-contain"
/&gt;</code></pre>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Attribute</th>
      <th>Purpose</th>
      <th>Why It Is Required</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>autoPlay</code></td>
      <td>Starts playback automatically</td>
      <td>Ensures the user doesn't need to manually click play.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>muted</code></td>
      <td>Mutes audio initially</td>
      <td>Required by Google Chrome, Safari, and Firefox to permit autoplay without user interaction.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>loop</code></td>
      <td>Repeats video playback</td>
      <td>Keeps milestone video active in the background.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>playsInline</code></td>
      <td>Plays inside the card/modal</td>
      <td>Prevents iOS/Safari mobile devices from forcing full-screen video takeover.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>controls</code></td>
      <td>Shows player controls</td>
      <td>Allows user to unmute, scrub timeline, pause, or enter full-screen.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>5. Lightbox Media Navigation: Arrow Buttons Strictly Slide Media</h2>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">Arrow Navigation Behavior:</div>
  <p>
    The prominent <strong>&lt;</strong> and <strong>&gt;</strong> arrow buttons on the media container are tied directly to <code>mediaItemIndex</code> (via <code>handlePrevMedia</code> and <code>handleNextMedia</code>).
    Clicking these arrows <strong>only slides between the photos and videos of the current memory</strong>. 
    The memory details (title, milestone story, uploader, date, tags, and category) remain firmly on the active memory and do <strong>not</strong> switch.
  </p>
  <p>
    If the user wants to switch to a different memory entirely, they can use the dedicated memory switcher buttons in the top header bar (<code>Memory X / Y</code>).
  </p>
</div>

